/-!
# The Lubin–Tate levels are finite over the ground field

The Lubin–Tate level `K_n = F(Λ_n)` is, by definition, the subextension of a fixed algebraic
closure `F̄` generated by the `πⁿ`-division points `Λ_n`, i.e. by the roots of the iterate
`f^{(n)}` of the standard Lubin–Tate polynomial `f(T) = πT + T^q` (Milne, *CFT*, §I.3).

Since the roots of a single polynomial generate a *finite* extension — indeed a splitting field,
hence of degree at most `(deg f)^{#roots}` — each level `K_n` is finite dimensional over `F`.
This file records that general fact (`Polynomial.finiteDimensional_adjoin_rootSet`) and applies
it to `LTLevel`.

## Main results

* `Polynomial.finiteDimensional_adjoin_rootSet`;
* `NK.LocalReciprocityLubinTate.LubinTate.finiteDimensional_LTLevel`.
-/

open Polynomial

namespace NK.LocalReciprocityLubinTate.LubinTate

universe u

noncomputable section

/-- **The extension generated by the roots of a polynomial is finite over the base field.**

If `L` is any field extension of `K` in which a polynomial `p : K[X]` splits — for instance if `L`
is algebraically closed — then the intermediate field `K(p.rootSet L)` generated by the roots of
`p` is finite dimensional over `K`.  Indeed `K(p.rootSet L)` is a splitting field of `p`, so it is
generated by the (finitely many) roots of `p`, each of which is algebraic over `K`.

No non-vanishing hypothesis on `p` is needed: for `p = 0` the root set is empty and the claim is
`FiniteDimensional K K`. -/
theorem finiteDimensional_adjoin_rootSet {K L : Type*} [Field K] [Field L] [Algebra K L]
    (p : K[X]) (hsplit : (p.map (algebraMap K L)).Splits) :
    FiniteDimensional K ↥(IntermediateField.adjoin K (p.rootSet L)) := by
  have : Polynomial.IsSplittingField K ↥(IntermediateField.adjoin K (p.rootSet L)) p :=
    IntermediateField.adjoin_rootSet_isSplittingField hsplit
  exact Polynomial.IsSplittingField.finiteDimensional _ p

/-- **The Lubin–Tate level `K_n = F(Λ_n)` is finite over `F`.**

It is generated by the roots in `AlgebraicClosure F` of the iterate `f^{(n)}` of the standard
Lubin–Tate polynomial, i.e. by the `πⁿ`-division points of the Lubin–Tate group, hence a splitting
field of `f^{(n)}` and therefore finite dimensional. -/
theorem finiteDimensional_LTLevel (F : Type u) [Field F] [ValuativeRel F] [TopologicalSpace F]
    [IsNonarchimedeanLocalField F] (π : F) (q n : ℕ) (hq : 2 ≤ q) :
    FiniteDimensional F ↥(NK.LocalReciprocityLubinTate.LTLevel F π q n) := by
  have hq' : 2 ≤ q := hq
  exact finiteDimensional_adjoin_rootSet (NK.LocalReciprocityLubinTate.LTpolyIter π q n)
    (IsAlgClosed.splits _)

end

end NK.LocalReciprocityLubinTate.LubinTate
